Q: Why do these 4 different random number generator functions produce the same series of numbers? A: <h3>The simplest fix</h3>
<p>The primary reason that function <code>randDouble</code> (from the OP) is not working is because it creates (and seeds) a new <em>random number engine</em> every time it is called.</p>
<p>The simplest way to fix this is to declare the engine to have <em>static storage duration</em>, by prefacing its declaration with the keyword <code>static</code>. This is the solution suggested by @Robert Andrzejuk, in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/48732245/22193627#comment84467988_48732245">his comment</a>. This change, <em>alone</em>, is enough to get the OP's program working.</p>
<p>Focusing on the OP's first attempt at coding <code>randDouble</code>, this change produces:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>double Network::randDouble(double low, double high) {
    static std::default_random_engine generator(
        static_cast&lt;unsigned int&gt;(
            std::chrono::system_clock::now().time_since_epoch().count()));
    std::uniform_real_distribution&lt;double&gt; distribution(low, high);
    return distribution(generator);
}
</code></pre>
<p>When declared <code>static</code>, the engine is initialized (i.e., seeded) only once, during the first call to function <code>randDouble</code>. The static engine is not deallocated until program teardown, <em>after</em> function <code>main</code> ends. In this regard, it is like a &quot;global&quot; variable; it retains its value (i.e., its internal state), between calls to function <code>randDouble</code>.</p>
<h3>Clock time may be a poor choice of seed</h3>
<p>Another thing that may be causing problems is the OP's use of current time as a seed. This problem goes away when the random number engine is declared <code>static</code>, as above, but it might be causing trouble in the OP's original version of <code>randDouble</code>.</p>
<p>Here's why:</p>
<p>The OP's version of <code>randDouble</code> constructs a new engine, and seeds it with the current time, every time it is invoked. When <code>randDouble</code> is called many times, in rapid succession, there is a significant chance that the time values used as seeds are not very different from each other. In the worst case, some of them could actually be the same.</p>
<p>The number of seconds between clock &quot;ticks&quot; of <code>std::chrono::system_clock</code> is given by <code>std::chrono::system_clock::period</code>. The period is an implementation-dependent quantity.</p>
<p>With clock rates between, say, 2 GHz and 6 GHz, modern personal computers execute several billions of instructions per second.</p>
<p>Depending on the clock rate of the computer in use, and also on the period of <code>std::chrono::system_clock</code>, function <code>randDouble</code> could be called <em>many</em> times, during a single &quot;tick&quot; of <code>std::chrono::system_clock</code>. If so, each of those invocations will use the same seed, and return the same &quot;random&quot; value.</p>
<h3>Other issues</h3>
<p>Simply declaring the random number engine to be <code>static</code> is enough to fix function <code>randDouble</code>.</p>
<p>It might be further improved by making one or more of the following changes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use <code>std::mt19937</code>, or some other engine that has a known implementation. This could improve the quality of random values, and improve program portability.</li>
<li>Seed the engine with <code>std::random_device</code>, rather than clock time. Seeding is a complex issue. This <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/80002015/22193627">Stack Overflow answer</a> discusses some of the trade-offs between using clock time, <code>std::random_device</code>, or some other technique, to generate random seeds.</li>
<li>Use <code>param_type</code> to pass a new set of bounds into the distribution object. That way, the distribution can be given static storage duration, and you don't have to construct a new distribution object every time <code>randDouble</code> is called.</li>
<li>Remove function <code>randDouble</code> from class <code>Network</code>, so that it becomes a <em>free function</em>. Otherwise, delcare function <code>randDouble</code> to be a <code>static</code> function within class <code>Network</code> (which the OP may already have done).</li>
</ul>
<p>Implementing all of these suggestions yields the following free-standing function:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>double random_double(double const a, double const b) {
    static std::mt19937 e{
        []()
        {
            std::random_device rd;
            std::seed_seq seed{ rd(), rd(), rd(), rd(), rd(), rd(), rd(), rd() };
            return std::mt19937{ seed };  // RVO is guaranteed.
        }()
    };
    static std::uniform_real_distribution&lt;double&gt; d;
    using param_type = typename std::uniform_real_distribution&lt;double&gt;::param_type;
    return d(e, param_type{ a, b });
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that <code>std::mt19937</code> is seeded by invoking an <em>immediately executed lambda</em>. The lambda constructs, and returns, a seeded engine, using <em>return value optimization</em> (RVO).</p>
<p>RVO guarantees that the lambda's engine is not copied during the return process. Instead, the lambda constructs its engine directly at the location where the returned engine would have been copied, if RVO were not in effect.</p>
<p>This is a significant efficiency gain, because a <code>std::mt19937</code> engine holds 624 state variables, which, otherwise, would have to be copied.</p>

Q: How to record audio from microphone and speaker in ReactJS with TypeScript and send it to the server for transcription? A: <p>I have a solution based on @Saad's comment. This also used the same approach but in JSX format. One issue I faced was, I had to use a button to share the audio from another tab. I added it here.</p>
<pre><code>import React, { useEffect, useRef } from &quot;react&quot;;

const TransComp = () =&gt; {
  const statusRef = useRef(null);
  const transcriptRef = useRef(null);
  const socketRef = useRef(null);
  const audioCtx = useRef(null);

  const startRecording = () =&gt; {
    if (audioCtx.current.state === &quot;suspended&quot;) {
      audioCtx.current.resume();
    }

    let mediaRecorder;

    const dest = audioCtx.current.createMediaStreamDestination();

    Promise.all([
      navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ audio: true }),
      navigator.mediaDevices.getDisplayMedia({
        video: { cursor: &quot;always&quot; },
        audio: true,
      }),
    ])
      .then(([micStream, displayStream]) =&gt; {
        if (!MediaRecorder.isTypeSupported(&quot;audio/webm&quot;)) {
          alert(&quot;Browser not supported&quot;);
          return;
        }

        [micStream, displayStream].forEach((str) =&gt; {
          const src = audioCtx.current.createMediaStreamSource(str);
          src.connect(dest);
        });

        mediaRecorder = new MediaRecorder(dest.stream, {
          mimeType: &quot;audio/webm&quot;,
        });

        if (!socketRef.current) {
          socketRef.current = new WebSocket(&quot;ws://localhost:5555/listen&quot;);
        }

        socketRef.current.onopen = () =&gt; {
          if (statusRef.current) statusRef.current.textContent = &quot;Connected&quot;;
          mediaRecorder.addEventListener(&quot;dataavailable&quot;, (event) =&gt; {
            if (event.data.size &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; socketRef.current.readyState === 1) {
              socketRef.current.send(event.data);
            }
          });
          mediaRecorder.start(250); //sending blobs of data every 250ms
        };

        socketRef.current.onmessage = (message) =&gt; {
          const received = message.data;
          console.log(received);
          if (received &amp;&amp; transcriptRef.current) {
            transcriptRef.current.textContent += &quot; &quot; + received;
          }
        };
      })
      .catch((error) =&gt; {
        console.error(&quot;Error:&quot;, error);
      });
  };

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    audioCtx.current = new AudioContext();
  }, []);

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    return () =&gt; {
      if (socketRef.current) {
        socketRef.current.close();
      }
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={startRecording}&gt;Start&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;div id=&quot;status&quot; ref={statusRef} /&gt;
      &lt;div id=&quot;transcript&quot; ref={transcriptRef} /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
};

export default TransComp;

</code></pre>

Q: angular material date-time picker - format the input with date and 24 hour time A: <p><strong>I think this would help you..</strong></p>
<p>You should set one option <strong><code>enableMeridian</code></strong>.<br />
Because this <code>enableMeridian</code> means : <code>Whether to display 12H or 24H mode.</code> and it's value if <code>true or false</code>. By default it is <code>false</code>..</p>
<p>So I think your code will be like this ..</p>
<pre><code>const CUSTOM_DATE_FORMATS: NgxMatDateFormats = {
  parse: {
    dateInput: &quot;l, LTS&quot;
  },
  display: {
    dateInput: &quot;l, LTS&quot;,
    monthYearLabel: &quot;MMM YYYY&quot;,
    dateA11yLabel: &quot;LL&quot;,
    monthYearA11yLabel: &quot;MMMM YYYY&quot;,
    enableMeridian: true
  }
};
</code></pre>
<p><strong>You can refer this link.</strong><br />
<a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@angular-material-components/datetime-picker" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.npmjs.com/package/@angular-material-components/datetime-picker</a></p>
